── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #1009  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Zicfiss instructions had no operation() spec: multi-level SSE gating on menvcfg/senvcfg/henvcfg needed per privilege mode to prevent shadow stack bypass via unchecked push/pop/amo/read-SSP in disabled or virtualized contexts
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:224-225  ENVCFG_SSE
      Correctly defines SSE at bit 3 per the RISC-V privileged spec. The constant itself is a faithful encoding of the architectural definition.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:62-75  set_shstk_status
      Correctly gates host U-mode shadow stack by writing ENVCFG_SSE into CSR_ENVCFG (senvcfg in S-mode). Checks is_user_shstk_enabled() (ISA extension + cmdline) before proceeding. Both the ubcfi_en flag and the hardware SSE bit are set atomically together. The function is correct for its scope — the gap is that no equivalent exists for KVM henvcfg/guest VS/VU gating, but that is a missing-feature issue in a different subsystem, not a bug in this function.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:123-152  amo_user_shstk
      Executes ssamoswap.d (Zicfiss instruction) without explicitly re-reading CSR_ENVCFG to verify SSE is still set. Relies entirely on hardware to trap if SSE=0. The function is always called after set_shstk_status() has set SSE, but there is no software-level defense-in-depth re-verification. If a TOCTOU window exists where SSE is cleared between enable and this instruction, and if a hardware implementation diverges on the enable-check sequence (the vulnerability pattern described in the spec), the instruction's side effects execute without SSE gating. The __enable_user_access()/__disable_user_access() pair only controls SMAP/PAN, not the instruction-level SSE gate.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:543-576  kvm_riscv_vcpu_setup_config
      Configures henvcfg bits for PBMTE, STCE, CBIE, CBCFE, CBZE, ADUE based on guest ISA extensions, but has NO ENVCFG_SSE propagation for Zicfiss. This means guest VS/VU-mode shadow-stack instructions (sspush, sspopchk, ssamoswap, ssrdp) execute without henvcfg.SSE gating. The spec requires that henvcfg.SSE must gate Zicfiss instructions in VS/VU modes. This is the single point where the fix must land: add a check for riscv_isa_extension_available(isa, ZICFISS) and set cfg->henvcfg |= ENVCFG_SSE. Every downstream consumer of cfg->henvcfg (kvm_arch_vcpu_load, the swap-in path) is materially affected by this omission.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_insn.c:328-359  kvm_riscv_vcpu_virtual_insn
      The virtual-instruction dispatcher handles 16-bit insn==0 (unpriv read retry) and routes 32-bit SYSTEM opcode instructions to system_opcode_insn(). Zicfiss instructions (sspush, sspopchk, ssamoswap, ssrdp, c.sspush, c.sspopchk) are NOT matched anywhere in the instruction-function table (system_opcode_funcs[]). They fall through to truly_illegal_insn(), which injects EXC_INST_ILLEGAL instead of the spec-required EXC_VIRTUAL_INST_FAULT. When henvcfg.SSE=0 and hardware correctly traps a Zicfiss instruction as EXC_VIRTUAL_INST_FAULT, KVM incorrectly converts it to EXC_INST_ILLEGAL, violating the architectural requirement that VS/VU-mode Zicfiss instructions must raise VirtualInstruction when henvcfg.SSE is 0. Additionally, 16-bit compressed forms (c.sspush, c.sspopchk) hit the INSN_IS_16BIT(insn) early-return at line 349 which unconditionally calls truly_illegal_insn().
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_insn.c:46-60  truly_virtual_insn
      The helper itself is correctly implemented: it sets scause=EXC_VIRTUAL_INST_FAULT and redirects the trap to the guest. The function works as designed. It is unreachable for Zicfiss faults only because the dispatcher (kvm_riscv_vcpu_virtual_insn) never matches Zicfiss opcodes and thus never returns KVM_INSN_VIRTUAL_TRAP for them. The bug is in the caller's opcode-matching logic, not in this function.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_sbi_fwft.c:66-73  kvm_fwft_defined_features
      Lists SBI_FWFT_SHADOW_STACK as a defined FWFT feature, advertising shadow-stack capability to guests via SBI. However, KVM does not mediate this against henvcfg.SSE — the enablement/enforcement path is entirely absent. A guest can observe Zicfiss availability through this SBI interface, attempt to use shadow-stack instructions, and hit the gaps in kvm_riscv_vcpu_setup_config and kvm_riscv_vcpu_virtual_insn. The feature-advertisement path exists without the corresponding enforcement path. Currently safe only if the guest never acts on the advertisement, which is not an invariant KVM enforces.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/uapi/asm/kvm.h:119-198  KVM_RISCV_ISA_EXT_ID
